Q: What is the correct Content Type of an .xps file A: <p>For an <code>.xps</code> file the MIME type is:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>application/vnd.ms-xpsdocument
</code></pre>
<p>For the newer <code>.oxps</code> format it is:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>application/oxps
</code></pre>
<p>If you need to configure IIS to serve <code>.xps</code> files with the correct MIME type, the corresponding <code>web.config</code> entry is:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;configuration&gt;
  &lt;system.webServer&gt;
    &lt;staticContent&gt;
      &lt;remove fileExtension=&quot;.xps&quot; /&gt;
      &lt;mimeMap fileExtension=&quot;.xps&quot;
               mimeType=&quot;application/vnd.ms-xpsdocument&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/staticContent&gt;
  &lt;/system.webServer&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>There is a MIME type reference here that lets you search by extension or MIME type and also generates the corresponding configuration for IIS and other web servers: <a href="https://ittools.tech/en/reference/mime-types" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://ittools.tech/en/reference/mime-types</a></p>
<p>One important point regarding the security part of the question: the <code>Content-Type</code> supplied with an uploaded file should not be considered reliable validation, since it can be set arbitrarily by the client.</p>
<p>If you really need to verify that the uploaded file is an XPS document, you should inspect/open the package and validate its structure rather than relying only on the filename or the HTTP <code>Content-Type</code>.</p>
